    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                "login": "echasnovski",
                "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Judging by your examples, you either use 'tpope/vim-surround' or setup of 'mini.surround' providing similar experience. By default all that examples give surrounding without padding (and `S[` doesn't even work).\r\n\r\nIn [setup of 'mini.surround' similar to 'vim-surround'](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c820abfe561a5946e1b327ff9912491876cbf366/doc/mini.txt#L4239-L4267) opening characters add pads and closing - don't. This is the most straightforward and suggested way of managing pads.\r\n\r\nIn theory, you should be able to write custom code that changes custom surroundings per filetype. Something along the lines of autocommands for specified events (`BufEnter`, `BufLeave`, `FileType`) which updates `MiniSurround.config.custom_surroundings`. However, this won't be added in module itself.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Judging by your examples, you either use 'tpope/vim-surround' or setup of 'mini.surround' providing similar experience. By default all that examples give surrounding without padding (and <code class=\"notranslate\">S[</code> doesn't even work).</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">In <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/c820abfe561a5946e1b327ff9912491876cbf366/doc/mini.txt#L4239-L4267\">setup of 'mini.surround' similar to 'vim-surround'</a> opening characters add pads and closing - don't. This is the most straightforward and suggested way of managing pads.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">In theory, you should be able to write custom code that changes custom surroundings per filetype. Something along the lines of autocommands for specified events (<code class=\"notranslate\">BufEnter</code>, <code class=\"notranslate\">BufLeave</code>, <code class=\"notranslate\">FileType</code>) which updates <code class=\"notranslate\">MiniSurround.config.custom_surroundings</code>. However, this won't be added in module itself.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Judging by your examples, you either use 'tpope/vim-surround' or setup of 'mini.surround' providing similar experience. By default all that examples give surrounding without padding (and S[ doesn't even work).\nIn setup of 'mini.surround' similar to 'vim-surround' opening characters add pads and closing - don't. This is the most straightforward and suggested way of managing pads.\nIn theory, you should be able to write custom code that changes custom surroundings per filetype. Something along the lines of autocommands for specified events (BufEnter, BufLeave, FileType) which updates MiniSurround.config.custom_surroundings. However, this won't be added in module itself.",
